SEO / GEO — Best Practices 2026
Reference file injected by the Reachizer orchestrator into Organic sub-agents (blog post, product page).
Last updated: June 2026

1. The 2026 Fundamental Shift: Two Engines to Feed
In 2026, organic visibility is no longer played out solely on Google. A growing share of searches goes through generative engines (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, Google AI Overviews) which no longer return a list of links — they synthesize a direct answer.
Two complementary disciplines, not opposing ones:
SEO	GEO
Optimize to be found by Google	Optimize to be cited by AIs
Objective: appear in the top 10 results	Objective: be the source extracted in the response
Signals: backlinks, Core Web Vitals, structure	Signals: factual density, author authority, citable structure
Fundamental rule: no GEO without healthy SEO. A technically broken site will neither rank well nor be cited. SEO remains the foundation.
What has changed: "Good enough" content is no longer sufficient. You must be the best answer available — for Google AND for AIs.

2. Technical SEO — The Non-Negotiable Foundation
Core Web Vitals (Google Ranking Factor)
Google measures real user experience via 3 metrics:
Metric	Target Threshold	What It Measures
LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)	< 2.5 seconds	Loading speed of main content
INP (Interaction to Next Paint)	< 200 ms	Responsiveness to user interactions
CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)	< 0.1	Visual stability (moving elements)
VSI (Visual Stability Index)	New 2026	Stability across the entire session (scroll, interactions)
Measurement tools: Google Search Console (real field data), PageSpeed Insights, Lighthouse.
Improvements become visible in rankings within 4 to 8 weeks (28-day data window).
Mobile-First Indexing
Google indexes and ranks the mobile version with priority. 60%+ of searches are done on mobile.
* Test with the "Mobile-Friendly Test" tool in Google Search Console
* Verify that content and tags are identical on mobile and desktop
Indexing and Crawlability
* Up-to-date XML Sitemap, submitted in Search Console
* Correct Robots.txt (do not accidentally block key pages)
* No duplicate content → canonical tags
* Resolve 404 errors and redirect chains
* Crawl budget: do not waste it on low-value pages (filter pages, infinite pagination)
Structured Data (Schema.org)
Mandatory for both SEO AND GEO. Allows engines to understand and extract content.
Priority schemas:
* Article: title, author, date, image → strong E-E-A-T signal
* FAQPage: question/answer pairs directly extractable by AIs
* Organization: name, logo, URL, social networks
* BreadcrumbList: structured navigation
* Product / Service: for product and service pages
Validate with: Rich Results Test (Google) + Schema Markup Validator
A poorly formed schema is worse than no schema at all.

3. SEO Content Strategy
Search Intent Above All
Each page = one specific intent. Four types of intent:
* Informational: "how X works" → blog article, guide
* Navigational: "X website" → homepage, brand page
* Commercial: "best X for Y" → comparison page, use case
* Transactional: "buy X", "X price" → product page, landing page
Never target two different intents on the same page.
Topic Cluster Architecture
Organize content around pillar pages linked to satellite articles.
Pillar page: "Marketing automation for SMEs"
  ├── Article: "How to automate B2B emails"
  ├── Article: "The best marketing automation tools"
  ├── Article: "Cost of a marketing automation solution"
  └── Article: "Marketing automation vs CRM: what is the difference?"
Each satellite article reinforces the pillar page through internal linking. Google and AIs perceive exhaustive topical coverage as an authority signal.
Internal Linking
* Systematically link satellite articles back to the pillar page (and to each other)
* Descriptive and natural anchor text (not "click here")
* Click depth: important pages must be accessible in a maximum of 3 clicks from the homepage
* Regularly check for broken links
Content Length and Depth
* Long-form and specialized content generates more natural backlinks and AI citations
* No arbitrary target length: be more complete than the positioned competitors
* Include what only a practicing expert can bring: real pitfalls, edge cases, field data
Content Freshness
* Update existing articles with new data (more effective than creating new ones)
* Indicate the update date → freshness signal for Google and AIs
* 2025/2026 dated content with sources → much more citable
4. E-E-A-T — Quality Signal for Google AND GEO
E-E-A-T (Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, Trustworthiness) is the primary quality filter for Google and LLMs. Content without real expertise signals will be disadvantaged in both systems.
How to signal E-E-A-T concretely
Experience:
* Concrete examples named with dates and results
* Data from real usage ("tested on 50 campaigns between January and March 2026")
* Documented errors with cause and correction
Expertise:
* Terminological precision (exact terms, no approximations)
* Coverage of edge cases and exceptions
* References to primary sources (studies, official documentation)
* FAQ addressing advanced questions, not basic ones
Authoritativeness:
* Complete author page with photo, biography, LinkedIn profile links
* Schema.org Person or Organization on each page
* Mentions in third-party media, industry publications
* Backlinks from recognized sites in the industry
Trustworthiness:
* Cited and linked sources (studies, statistics with date and origin)
* Verifiable figures and data
* Detailed "About Us" page, legal notices, clear T&Cs
* HTTPS, GDPR-compliant privacy policy
5. GEO — Optimizing to be Cited by AIs
How LLMs Select Their Sources
Generative engines look for the densest passage that answers the question. They favor:
1. Sources with good classic SEO (indexed, ranked)
2. Structured content (clear headings, FAQs, lists)
3. Factual, dated, and sourced statements
4. Sources with visible E-E-A-T signals
5. Consistent brands present across multiple channels
"Citable" Content — The Rules
BLUF Principle (Bottom Line Up Front):
The answer to the question must be in the very first sentence. No 3-paragraph context-setting before getting to the point.
❌ Bad: "For several years, companies have been looking for solutions to optimize their online presence..."
✅ Good: "GEO (Generative Engine Optimization) is the set of techniques that optimize content to be cited by AI engines like ChatGPT or Perplexity."
Citable Chunks:
A passage of 40 to 80 words containing:
* A clear statement
* A number or a date
* An identifiable source or context → infinitely more citable than a 200-word descriptive paragraph without a factual anchor.
What makes content citable:
* Sourced and dated figures: "In 2026, 68% of SMEs..."
* Structured lists: AIs easily extract hierarchical information
* Comparison tables: format natively readable by LLMs
* Clear definitions at the beginning of a section
* Structured FAQ (FAQPage schema): question/answer format = native LLM format
llms.txt File
New 2026 standard (inspired by robots.txt) to indicate to LLMs which pages to crawl and cite.
Place at the root of the site: [https://mysite.com/llms.txt](https://mysite.com/llms.txt)
Content: list of important pages, descriptions, instructions for AI crawlers.
Multichannel Presence
Distributing your content across multiple channels (LinkedIn, industry newsletters, specialized press) increases AI citations by up to 325% compared to publishing solely on your site. LLMs perceive multi-source entity consistency as a strong authority signal.
AI-Friendly Robots.txt
Verify that LLM crawlers (GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot) are not blocked in robots.txt — unless there is a deliberate desire not to be indexed by AIs.
6. Backlinks and Domain Authority
Netlinking remains the most powerful trust signal for Google in 2026.
Quality vs. Quantity Approach
* 1 link from a recognized industry site > 100 links from generic directories
* Editorial links (cited within a core article) > footer or sidebar links
How to Obtain Natural Backlinks
* "Linkable" content: original studies, exclusive data, free tools, comprehensive guides
* Guest posting on industry publications (with real editorial value)
* Digital PR: being cited as an expert in media articles
* Content partnerships with complementary players
* Testimonials and case studies on partner/supplier sites
What to Avoid
* Buying links (Google penalty)
* Massive link exchanges
* Links from topically unrelated sites
7. Measuring and Steering SEO/GEO
Essential Tools
* Google Search Console: free, indispensable. Impressions, clicks, positions, indexing issues, field Core Web Vitals
* Google Analytics 4: organic traffic, behavior, conversions
* PageSpeed Insights / Lighthouse: Core Web Vitals per page
* Ahrefs or Semrush: position tracking, backlink analysis, keyword opportunities
GEO Tracking — Manual Measurement
No tool measures visibility in LLMs perfectly. Recommended method:
1. Define 10 key questions your prospects ask in your industry
2. Ask them once a month in ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Google AI Overviews
3. Note if your brand/site is cited, and in what context
4. Objective: go from 0/10 to 5/10 citations over 6 months
SEO KPIs to Track
KPI	Tool	Frequency
Rankings on target keywords	Search Console / Ahrefs	Weekly
Organic traffic	GA4	Weekly
Core Web Vitals	Search Console	Monthly
Average Click-Through Rate (CTR)	Search Console	Monthly
Indexed pages	Search Console	Monthly
Backlink profile	Ahrefs / Semrush	Monthly
AI Citations (GEO)	Manual test	Monthly
8. Implementation Recommendations According to the Objective
Objective: Optimized SEO + GEO Blog Post
Recommended Structure:
1. H1 Title: include the main keyword + a question or benefit formulation
2. Introduction: answer the main question directly in 2-3 sentences (BLUF)
3. Table of contents with internal anchors (structure signal for LLMs)
4. Body: H2 per sub-theme, H3 for details. Alternate short paragraphs, lists, tables
5. Structured FAQ at the end of the article (FAQPage schema): 3 to 5 questions that prospects actually ask
6. Cited and linked sources
7. Article Schema with author, date, image
Checklist before publication:
* [ ] Search intent is identified and respected
* [ ] Main answer is in the first 100 words
* [ ] At least one dated and sourced figure per section
* [ ] Article + FAQPage schemas implemented
* [ ] Internal linking to the pillar page and 2-3 related articles
* [ ] Meta title (< 60 characters) and meta description (< 155 characters) written
* [ ] Images optimized (WebP format, descriptive alt text)
* [ ] Core Web Vitals score in the green on mobile
Objective: Product / Service SEO Page
Recommended Structure:
1. H1: main benefit + keyword (not just the product name)
2. Short description: what it is, who it is for, result obtained
3. Proof: figures, customer testimonials, logos, case studies
4. Functional details: list of features with associated benefits
5. FAQ on common objections (FAQPage schema)
6. Clear and repeated CTA
E-E-A-T Signals on a Product Page:
* Number of clients, countries, years of existence
* Verified reviews with names and companies
* Certifications, partnerships, known integrations
9. AI Content — Publication Rules
AI-generated content must be validated and enriched by a human expert before publication. Google detects and penalizes raw AI content without added value.
What needs to be added to AI-generated content:
* Field data that only a practitioner can provide
* Concrete examples named with dates and results
* An expert angle or opinion that cannot be found elsewhere
* Proofreading to remove generic phrasing and typical AI turns of phrase
What remains prohibited:
* AI content published without proofreading
* Keyword stuffing
* Copying or paraphrasing existing articles
10. Common Errors to Avoid
Error	Impact	Correction
No schema.org	Not citable by LLMs, fewer Google rich snippets	Implement Article + FAQPage as a priority
Homepage as the target for all links	Poorly distributed authority, deep pages under-ranked	Topical internal linking to key pages
Content without clear intent	Google does not know who to rank → poor position	One page = one intent = one main keyword
Ignoring mobile	Degraded ranking (mobile-first indexing)	Systematically test and correct on mobile
Blocking GPTBot / ClaudeBot in robots.txt	Invisible to LLMs	Check and allow AI crawlers
Raw AI content published without enrichment	Google algorithmic penalty, not citable	Mandatory validation + expert enrichment
Updating positions but not the content	Content ages, loses GEO authority	Quarterly content audit + data update
No author page	Missing E-E-A-T signal → less cited by AIs	Author page + Person schema on each article
Targeting low-volume keywords on a new site	No traffic despite efforts	Start with the long tail, then move up in competitiveness
No external presence (press, LinkedIn, newsletter)	GEO authority limited to the site alone	Distribute content on other channels
